(This is a very interesting problem! Let me first explain what the SRB) Tj
T* (pins do and what the BIAS pin does. The SRB pins are essentially inputs) Tj
T* (for a reference input that you intend to connect to multiple channels.) Tj
T* (The SRB2 pin can be connected to individual channels' positive inputs.) Tj
T* (The SRB1 pin can be connected to all channels' negative inputs.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The BIAS pin is the equivalent of a right-leg driver for an ECG measurement.) Tj
T* (It will output a signal that attempts to keep the patient's common-mode) Tj
T* (voltage within the valid range for the ADC.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (This datasheet was actually just updated with a helpful section that discusses) Tj
T* (the various functions of these pins. You can find it here starting on page 65.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (In your case, though, since it is intended to be so modular, I recommend) Tj
T* (you simply connect all INxN inputs to analog ground \(assuming you are) Tj
T* (using bipolar supplies\) and find each electrode voltage in a single-ended) Tj
T* (fashion. Then you can average each sample to calculate a "reference voltage" to then analyze each individual channel.) Tj
